{"id": 21132, "stem": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-text\"><span><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-100\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">A</mark> 2-year-old boy with no chronic medical conditions is brought to the emergency department after he was found unresponsive with vomitus in his mouth.\u00a0 The patient has had muscle aches, cough, rhinorrhea, diarrhea, vomiting, and fever for the past 3 days.\u00a0 His parents gave him aspirin twice daily for symptom control.\u00a0 Vital signs are within normal limits.\u00a0 On examination, the patient is somnolent but withdraws from pain.\u00a0 Pupils are equal in size and reactive to light.\u00a0 Cardiopulmonary examination shows no abnormalities.\u00a0 Hepatomegaly is present.\u00a0 Extremities are well perfused.\u00a0 Laboratory results are as follows:</p><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 1rem;\">Aspartate aminotransferase (SGOT)</td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 1rem;\">2,170 U/L</td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 1rem;\">Alanine aminotransferase (SGPT)</td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 1rem;\">1,587 U/L</td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 1rem;\">Total bilirubin</td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 1rem;\">1.7 mg/dL</td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 1rem;\">Direct bilirubin</td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 1rem;\">1.2 mg/dL</td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 1rem;\">Serum ammonia</td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 1rem;\">106 \u03bcmol/L (normal: 11-32)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Over the next day, the patient's condition deteriorates and he dies despite supportive care.\u00a0 Examination of the brain would most likely reveal which of the following findings?\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b</p></span></div>", "options": [{"label": "A", "text": "Diffuse astrocyte swelling"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Dural venous thrombosis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Hemorrhagic necrosis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Inflammation of vessel walls"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Long-chain fatty acid deposits"}, {"label": "F", "text": "Pontine myelinolysis"}], "correctAnswer": "A", "explanation": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-explanation\"><span class=\"\"><div id=\"div_t69965\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-50\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">R</mark>eye syndrome</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Pathophysiology</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Aspirin use in children during viral infection (eg,\u00a0influenza, varicella)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Mitochondrial toxicity \u2192 impaired fatty acid metabolism</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Microvesicular fat deposits in the liver</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Hepatic dysfunction \u2192 hyperammonemia</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Diffuse astrocyte swelling (ie, cerebral edema)</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Clinical features</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Acute liver failure<ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"list-style-type: circle; font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Hepatomegaly</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Elevated transaminases; coagulopathy</li></ul></li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Rapidly progressive encephalopathy<ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"list-style-type: circle; font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Vomiting, lethargy, seizures, coma</li></ul></li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Treatment</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Supportive</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">This patient had acute-onset <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">encephalopathy</strong> and <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">liver failure</strong> (eg, hepatomegaly, elevated transaminases) following the administration of aspirin for a <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">viral infection</strong> (eg, influenza).\u00a0 These findings are characteristic of <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Reye syndrome</strong>, a rapidly progressive and potentially fatal toxic-metabolic encephalopathy.\u00a0 Most cases are related to salicylates (eg, <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">aspirin</strong>), which can cause <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">mitochondrial toxicity</strong>, particularly when given to young children for virally-induced fever.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">In the liver, impaired fatty acid oxidation by mitochondria leads to <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-qsz6itvm6933f1am5kiiov6v\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-qsz6itvm6933f1am5kiiov6v\">fat deposition</a> in hepatocytes (ie, <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">hepatic steatosis</strong>), hepatomegaly, and acute liver failure.\u00a0 In turn, excess <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">ammonia accumulates</strong> due to hepatic dysfunction (eg, reduced urea cycle activity), causing <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">diffuse astrocyte swelling</strong> and cerebral edema (eg, vomiting, confusion, lethargy).</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Treatment of Reye syndrome is supportive.\u00a0 Elevated intracranial pressure (ICP), which disrupts cerebral perfusion, is a major cause of death.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice B)</strong>\u00a0 Dural venous thrombosis most commonly presents with headache in young women with hypercoagulable risk factors (eg, oral contraception).\u00a0 Encephalopathy due to increased ICP can occur in severe cases, but liver function would not be affected.\u00a0 This patient's age, elevated aminotransferases, and hyperammonemia make this diagnosis unlikely.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choices C and D)</strong>\u00a0 Hemorrhagic necrosis of the temporal lobe is characteristic of herpes simplex virus encephalitis, which can present with altered mental status and focal neurologic deficits similar to those of an inflammatory CNS vasculitis.\u00a0 However, hepatomegaly and acute liver failure would not be expected with either condition.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice E)</strong>\u00a0 Long-chain fatty acid deposition in the brain can be seen in patients with Alzheimer disease, which causes chronic dementia, not rapidly progressive encephalopathy.\u00a0 Although serum fatty acids can be elevated with Reye syndrome, cerebral deposition does not occur.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice F)</strong>\u00a0 Pontine myelinolysis (osmotic demyelination syndrome) can cause confusion, lethargy, and coma but typically occurs with rapid correction of hyponatremia due to shifting of water out of brain cells.\u00a0 Liver dysfunction is not associated with this condition.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>Reye syndrome should be suspected in a young child with acute liver failure and encephalopathy after receiving aspirin for a virus-induced fever.\u00a0 Aspirin in this setting is a mitochondrial toxin that leads to hepatic steatosis, hyperammonemia, and diffuse astrocyte swelling (ie, cerebral edema).\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "Reye syndrome should be suspected in a young child with acute liver failure and encephalopathy after receiving aspirin for a virus-induced fever.  Aspirin in this setting is a mitochondrial toxin that leads to hepatic steatosis, hyperammonemia, and diffuse astrocyte swelling (ie, cerebral edema).\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u2